TITLE
Distributed Design in Ptolemy II

CHAPTER
02.cad

ABSTRACT

The advantages of distributed computing architectures can be 
seen in a wide array of applications. The advantages include: 
<OL>
<LI>Facilities for collaboration between physically separated 
users.
<LI>Secure access to remote resources.
<LI>Parallel execution to reduce computation duration.
</OL>
<P>
The response of the CAD community to the rise of distributed 
applications must be a two pronged approach. First, CAD tools 
should themselves be distributed to leverage the benefits that 
distributed architectures provide. Second, CAD tools should 
incorporate facilities for effectively designing distributed 
applications.
<P>
The Common Object Request Broker Architecture (CORBA) is a
specification for distributed software systems that allows 
software objects to communicate with one another independent 
of their implementation language. We are exploring the use of
CORBA to add distributed capabilities to the Ptolemy II 
infrastructure. In addition, we are considering methods of
using Ptolemy II to design and deploy distributed systems.
Ptolemy II is a design environment that emphasizes the use of 
hierarchical, heterogeneous specification semantics for the 
design of complex computational systems.
<P>
In Ptolemy II, we have demonstrated the feasibility to import a 
remote system as a component of a model, and execute the model 
over a network. It is still an open issue as to what is the smallest 
set of information that is needed to cross the boundary between local
and remote components. Other issues that must be dealt with include
real-time support and optimal underlying communication protocols.
